"""The "Why it is here" paragraphs of the instructions of group mn-ext, which the prefix-coded encoding adds (tools/manual/isa_part4.py).  As in isa_stories.py:
written from the record only (the proposals and the measurements of the repository); where no reason is recorded the text says so."""

FUSED = ('I decided the four fused pairs (`movcall`, `popmret`, `popret`, `mov2`) and left the fusing to the compiler: the assembler never fuses. '
         'The reason is the measurement of `docs/encoding-proposal.md` section 8 (static size of the training corpus, in the encoding): ')

STORY = {
'MOVCALL': FUSED + '`MOV Rs, Rd` followed by `CALL` occurs 11,123 times, and fusing it alone shrinks the code by 2.39%, the largest of the four; all four together fuse 20,428 pairs and save 3.18% of the bytes and about 1.1% of the executed instructions. The call keeps its reach (four or six bytes). The cost, named in the encoding study: a new mnemonic, and one instruction that writes two registers (the destination and the stack pointer), which is two micro-operations for an out-of-order core. The record gives no earlier machine for this particular pair; the reasoning is the fourth priority of the definitive list, fewer instructions executed, where coalescing is good.',
'POPRET': FUSED + '`POP Rd` followed by `RET` and `POPM` followed by `RET` (`POPMRET`) occur 4,862 times together and save 1.00% of the bytes; this is the epilogue of nearly every function. The fault of the second half restarts the whole instruction, which is idempotent because the first half reads a register the second does not change and `POPM` is already specified restartable (decision 2 of the encoding study).',
'POPMRET': 'See `POPRET`: the two are measured together (4,862 pairs, 1.00% of the bytes). The mask is an 8-bit field scaled by 256 because one parcel has no room for a wider one; the epilogue that restores `R8` to `R15` is the common case the corpus shows, and any other set stays `POPM` and `RET`.',
'MOV2': FUSED + '`MOV Ra, Rb` followed by `MOV Rc, Rd` occurs 6,742 times and saves 0.50%, the smallest gain of the four and the dearest in code space: four 3-bit register fields use one sixteenth of the first-parcel space and can name only the eight hot registers. The encoding study lists both as costs, and one instruction that writes two registers (the rename width of an out-of-order core).',
'CT': 'I asked for range checks on a register value and decided a trap-if-zero, a two-bound check and a compare-and-trap with a table of check sites; the names and forms were accepted on 2026-11-17 and the prefix optimiser placed them. The census of the corpus showed that every check of a subscript is an unsigned compare against an immediate that fits 12 bits, which is why the immediate form is 12 bits and why the table, not a stub, says which check failed.',
'CHK2': 'The two-bound check: an index with a lower bound other than zero is one instruction instead of a subtraction and a compare. It is signed and half-open, as pcc\'s built-in and a C range are.',
'MASK': 'I adopted the branch-free select when the crypto study asked for it: constant-time code must choose between two values without a branch, and a flag-reading conditional move would put a secret in a flag. `MASKEQZ` and `MASKNEZ` are the select of LoongArch and of RISC-V Zicond (stated from memory in the study, not checked against a manual); the pair and an `OR` make `c ? a : b` in three instructions, against two for `CMP` and a conditional move, but the conditional move would be used at 783 sites in 284,641 instructions (0.28%), and the case for the pair is the constant-time idiom, where the condition is already a register. I confirmed the pair and wrote "looks good" on the section. About 100 look-up tables, one cycle (the study\'s estimate).',
'BSEL': 'A bit select takes three sources and a destination, the four register fields of the three-operand group. Its case in the crypto proposal is SHA-2 (`Ch` and `Maj` are one `BSEL` each) and the constant-time swap: the X25519 ladder swaps ten limbs per step, 50 instructions with the mask pair against about 25 with `BSEL`. It is the select of ARM (`BSL`) and of x86 ternary logic, and it serves MD5 and SHA-1 round functions, blends and bit insertion. It is one of the select instructions of the secure core.',
'OVF': 'Checked arithmetic is what clang `-ftrapv` and `__builtin_*_overflow`, Rust\'s debug overflow checks, Swift\'s default arithmetic and Ada\'s `Constraint_Error` need. I decided the fused `ADDV`, `SUBV` and `MULV`, as the case where one non-destructive instruction beats `ADD` and a check. The corpus cannot size the use: it contains no `BVS` or `BVC` at all, because the overflow builtins were not implemented in the compiler that produced it, and the study says that sanitiser builds are the target. The record gives the precedent only for the trap, not for the fused forms.',
'MCPYU': 'The block copy is how a kernel copies a buffer, and it had no user-qualified form, which was the reason to keep the status bit and its two instructions. It has two now, one for each direction, because a copy-in reads user memory and writes kernel memory and a copy-out the other way round; a qualifier that said both sides were user would fault on the kernel side.',
'AESR': 'Constant-time software AES takes about 1,000 instructions a block (BearSSL\'s bitsliced code, scaled; the proposal\'s estimate), and table AES leaks through the cache. I asked for constant-time AES and SHA instructions "or whichever opcodes would speed up and enable aes and sha" for the secure core. The design is RISC-V\'s scalar cryptography (`aes64es` and its kin): the state is in the general registers, so no vector register file is needed and, unlike the AES drivers of OpenBSD for x86 and ARM, no `fpu_kernel_enter()` bracket either: a kernel AES loop saves nothing and is preemptible with the ordinary register save. A block costs 42 instructions for AES-128, 20 to 25 times fewer than the software sequence (estimates). Precedent for the pair-in-registers shape: RISC-V; the other machines (x86 AES-NI, ARM `AESE`) use vector registers.',
'AESK': 'The key schedule and the inverse mix of the round key complete the round instructions: three instructions per round of the AES-128 schedule, about 30 for the whole schedule instead of about 150, once per key; `AESIM` adds 18 for the decryption schedule. The same precedent as `AESR`.',
'SHA256': 'The SHA-2 rounds are rotations and three-input exclusive ors, which the integer instructions do in several steps; the RISC-V scalar cryptography extension has the eight functions as instructions. The crypto proposal counts the gain honestly: the primitives remove half the work, not nineteen twentieths, because a round still needs the adds and the register traffic (about 1,200 against 2,500 instructions for a 64-byte block with `BSEL`, estimates).',
'SHA512': 'See `SHA256`: the four 64-bit functions of SHA-512, with the same gain of about two (about 1,500 against 3,100 instructions for a 128-byte block, estimates).',
'MSHA': 'Stage 2 of the crypto proposal, in the manner of the s390 CPACF instructions (`KIMD` and kin): one instruction hashes a buffer, the padding and chaining are done in hardware and so cannot be got wrong, and progress is held in registers, so a fault or an interrupt restarts it, as for `MCPY`. The estimate is 64 cycles for a 64-byte SHA-256 block against about 1,200 instructions, roughly 18 times; the cost is gates in proportion to the algorithms frozen into opcodes. The proposal says to build `MSHA256` first and the others only after measurement, and I have not decided it.',
'MAESCTR': 'Stage 2, as `MSHA`; whole-buffer AES gains less (about 4 times: 0.6 against 2.6 instructions per byte), because the round instructions are already good, and the proposal puts it after `MSHA256` and `MSHA512`.',
}
